57
00:06:53.820 --> 00:07:02.339
Barry Plunkett: So, for anyone who's not familiar, IBC stands for the Interblockchain Communication Protocol. It's a…

58
00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:13.939
Barry Plunkett: ledger interoperability solution that is application agnostic and network agnostic. It can connect, any two kinds of systems that.

59
00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:14.910
Barry Plunkett: 10.

60
00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:22.220
Barry Plunkett: sign, data that's typically used for blockchains and for DLT.

61
00:07:24.070 --> 00:07:40.760
Barry Plunkett: Matthew and I are here representing the Interchain Foundation, which is a non-profit foundation that has stewarded IBC for the last 7 years. IBC, along with a number of other technologies in the Cosmos ecosystem, for those who are familiar with it.

62
00:07:43.100 --> 00:07:47.750
Barry Plunkett: The Interchain Foundation originally built and funded IBC.

63
00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:51.320
Barry Plunkett: To try to help build.

64
00:07:51.510 --> 00:08:02.210
Barry Plunkett: a world where financial assets and markets could flow as easily between ledgers and applications and users as data flows on the internet.

65
00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:04.030
Barry Plunkett: we were…

66
00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:19.800
Barry Plunkett: among the first to start to think about and build for a multi-chain ledger environment back in 2017. So, interoperability was always going to be a big part of how we built

67
00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:27.170
Barry Plunkett: that world and how we tried to bring it into existence. And we were deeply inspired by…

68
00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:37.070
Barry Plunkett: The protocols that undergird the internet today, TCP/IP, meaning protocols that are peer-to-peer, that are standards-based.

69
00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:42.289
Barry Plunkett: That are application agnostic and permissionless to implement and deploy.

70
00:08:42.789 --> 00:08:49.839
Barry Plunkett: these were the ideas that inspired us when we started building IBC back in 2019. It…

71
00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:55.329
Barry Plunkett: has seen massive adoption in and around the Cosmos ecosystem.

72
00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:58.049
Barry Plunkett: So today, it connects 150 different

73
00:08:58.340 --> 00:09:15.760
Barry Plunkett: blockchains, or 190, depending on what data source you use. It counts many teams historically and currently as contributors, and it's secured $50 billion plus in transaction flows. But…

74
00:09:15.890 --> 00:09:23.360
Barry Plunkett: We really deeply feel that it hasn't Realized its full potential yet.

75
00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:24.659
Barry Plunkett: It is…

76
00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:40.489
Barry Plunkett: you know, despite our best efforts to make it and design it in a neutral way, still seen as a Cosmos solution that is controlled by the Cosmos ecosystem, and we think that that is holding it back from

77
00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:49.480
Barry Plunkett: Actually being able to help grow this vision of truly interoperable networks and assets that we want to contribute to.

78
00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:52.410
Barry Plunkett: And so we.

79
00:09:52.990 --> 00:10:03.910
Barry Plunkett: want to contribute it to the LFDT to unlock it, to really make it a more… to give it a more credibly neutral home that will allow it to attract

80
00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:12.679
Barry Plunkett: contributors from outside the Cosmos ecosystem and users from… from elsewhere with different needs and different interests, and

81
00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:15.120
Barry Plunkett: On fair and neutral ground.

82
00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:16.790
Barry Plunkett: And it's something that…

83
00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:35.810
Barry Plunkett: Even though we've only been talking about doing it over the past couple of months, we're really starting to already see there are teams now contributing light clients and integrations into ecosystems that we're not familiar with. And that really got kickstarted by their excitement around this kind of more neutral home that's coming.

84
00:10:37.450 --> 00:10:43.660
Barry Plunkett: And So that's why we're contributing IBC. I want to talk a little bit about

85
00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:45.900
Barry Plunkett: why I think it's a good fit.

86
00:10:46.150 --> 00:10:50.279
Barry Plunkett: From a technical perspective, and then I'll take questions.

87
00:10:50.870 --> 00:10:52.699
Barry Plunkett: The first reason is that.

88
00:10:52.890 --> 00:10:55.169
Barry Plunkett: IBC is open source.

89
00:10:55.290 --> 00:10:59.269
Barry Plunkett: end-to-end. That means not just the protocol, but also

90
00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:16.469
Barry Plunkett: all of the infrastructure that's built around it today. The relaying services that move the packets that make up the protocol, the signing services that attest to data on the different networks, and all of the different

91
00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:25.540
Barry Plunkett: CLIs and tools that plug into those. It's really designed to be a toolkit to allow teams to build the kinds of interoperability solutions that they need

92
00:11:25.700 --> 00:11:27.919
Barry Plunkett: While adhering to common standards.

93
00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:30.390
Barry Plunkett: To that end.

94
00:11:30.500 --> 00:11:43.909
Barry Plunkett: It has no token, there is no requirement to work with us or anyone else, there never has been, and there's no permissioning of the system. There are many users today who we haven't even met.

95
00:11:44.680 --> 00:11:52.879
Barry Plunkett: The second is it's standards-based. So, IBC refers to both the implementation of the protocol.

96
00:11:52.990 --> 00:11:58.969
Barry Plunkett: But also, a set of application level and…

97
00:11:59.070 --> 00:12:06.250
Barry Plunkett: Core level and security standards that describe how the data is structured.

98
00:12:06.980 --> 00:12:11.479
Barry Plunkett: how it can be passed off to applications in different contexts.

99
00:12:11.580 --> 00:12:20.949
Barry Plunkett: And, how it can be validated on different kinds of networks. And these standards are modular, meaning

100
00:12:21.370 --> 00:12:37.030
Barry Plunkett: They are agnostic to application type. There are numerous different application types that are implemented in IBC today. Smart contract calls, different mint and burn bridging mechanisms, different lock and mint mechanisms. It's agnostic to network, so…

101
00:12:37.070 --> 00:12:46.109
Barry Plunkett: There are standards that describe how to use IBC in different kinds of blockchain contexts, as well as some non-blockchain contexts.

102
00:12:46.470 --> 00:12:58.550
Barry Plunkett: And it's agnostic to security mechanism. So, there are standards for consensus-based light clients, as well as Oracle-based solutions for trusted signers to secure data.

103
00:12:59.130 --> 00:13:03.809
Barry Plunkett: And this design, these kinds of… this collection of modular standards.

104
00:13:03.880 --> 00:13:16.929
Barry Plunkett: is really helpful for facilitating collaboration with the other projects that are already in the Linux Foundation, integrations into Vesu at the kind of security and network layer, as well as

105
00:13:16.930 --> 00:13:24.929
Barry Plunkett: collaborations with, TACTI and some of the other, interoperability standards and, and.

106
00:13:25.550 --> 00:13:29.529
Barry Plunkett: solutions at the application and the asset layer.

107
00:13:30.050 --> 00:13:36.020
Barry Plunkett: We've started some of those conversations with the different maintainers here, and we're really excited to continue them.

108
00:13:36.630 --> 00:13:44.340
Barry Plunkett: And the final reason is that IBC is coming in as battle-tested. It's been around in production.

109
00:13:44.340 --> 00:13:58.420
Barry Plunkett: For five years, it's grown considerably over that time. It's widely deployed and used, and it's secured tens of billions of dollars of asset transfers on public networks, and potentially much more than that on private networks.

110
00:13:59.050 --> 00:14:13.269
Barry Plunkett: And so it gives the Linux Foundation, on the first day that it arrives, a battle-tested, widely used solution with a number of existing contributors, not just from the Interchain Foundation. And so.

111
00:14:14.250 --> 00:14:16.370
Barry Plunkett: For all those reasons, we think it's a great

112
00:14:16.510 --> 00:14:21.279
Barry Plunkett: candidate for, contribution to the LFDT.

113
00:14:22.180 --> 00:14:32.989
Barry Plunkett: And we're really excited to continue developing it and supporting it, after this transition. We… the team

114
00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:36.400
Barry Plunkett: The Interchain Foundation.

115
00:14:36.510 --> 00:14:54.430
Barry Plunkett: has been deeply committed to these ideas of open source for a long time, and we've long been inspired by the Linux Foundation, and we're really excited to continue building, in close partnership with the other folks and projects that are already here. So, with that, I will…

